    "Full API spec": "pom.xml: Maven build descriptor targeting Java 11. Declares dependency org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter (test scope). Configures maven-surefire-plugin for JUnit 5. No runtime dependencies.\n\nsrc/main/java/QRMatrix.java: class QRMatrix { public QRMatrix(int size); public int getSize(); public boolean get(int row, int col); public void set(int row, int col, boolean val); public boolean[][] copyCells(); }. Semantics: size immutable, cells are square size x size. Bounds checks throw IndexOutOfBoundsException. copyCells returns a deep copy. No IO.\n\nsrc/main/java/Checksum.java: class Checksum { public static int sumMod256(byte[] data); }. Semantics: sums every provided byte as unsigned (b & 0xFF), returns sum % 256. No side effects.\n\nsrc/main/java/ZigZag.java: class ZigZag { public static int[][] traversal(int size, boolean[][] reserved); }. Semantics: validates reserved is size x size. Two-pass bounded row-serpentine algorithm: First pass counts only unreserved cells with int count = 0; then literally allocates int[][] result = new int[count][2];. Second pass: for (int row = 0; row < size; row++) { if (row % 2 == 0) iterate col 0..size-1; else iterate col size-1..0; append coordinates only when !reserved[row][col]; }. No while loops, MUST NOT allocate new int[size * size][2], MUST NOT use index < size * size termination, and MUST NOT emit placeholder zeros. Consumers (encode/decode) use returned coordinates directly without re-filtering.\n\nsrc/main/java/FinderPattern.java: class FinderPattern { public static boolean[][] reservedMask(int size); public static void place(QRMatrix matrix); public static void verify(QRMatrix matrix); }. Semantics: Defines and enforces exactly three 7x7 finder squares at top-left, top-right, and bottom-left. reservedMask marks those cells true; throws IllegalArgumentException if size is even or < 21. place sets those cells to true in the given matrix. verify ensures those cells are true and reserved pattern matches exactly; throws IllegalArgumentException on mismatch.\n\nsrc/main/java/TextEncoder.java: class TextEncoder { public static byte[] encodePayload(String text); public static String decodePayload(byte[] frame); public static boolean[] bytesToBits(byte[] bytes); public static byte[] bitsToBytes(boolean[] bits); }. Semantics:\n- encodePayload: UTF-8 encodes text; builds frame [lenHi, lenLo, payload..., checksum]; computes checksum ONLY over the prefix (two length bytes plus payload) via java.util.Arrays.copyOf(frame, frame.length - 1) and Checksum.sumMod256; writes checksum as final byte; never computes over the complete frame.\n- decodePayload: requires frame length == 2 + L + 1; validates checksum using the same prefix-only java.util.Arrays.copyOf(frame, frame.length - 1) and compares to (frame[frame.length - 1] & 0xFF); decodes payload as strict UTF-8 and returns String. Throws IllegalArgumentException on length mismatch or checksum mismatch, and CharacterCodingException-derived errors are wrapped or rethrown as IllegalArgumentException.\n- bytesToBits: emits exactly eight MSB-first bits per byte using the literal statement bits[i * 8 + bit] = (bytes[i] & (1 << (7 - bit))) != 0;.\n- bitsToBytes: accepts any non-null bit array length (including 0 and non-multiples of 8). It must literally allocate byte[] result = new byte[(bits.length + 7) / 8]; and set each true bit with result[i / 8] |= (byte) (1 << (7 - (i % 8)));. No modulo-length rejection, exceptions, assertions, early returns, or truncation if bits.length % 8 != 0.\n\nsrc/main/java/MatrixValidator.java: class MatrixValidator { public static void ensureValidSize(int size); public static int capacityBits(int size); }. Semantics: ensureValidSize accepts odd sizes >= 21, else throws IllegalArgumentException. capacityBits uses FinderPattern.reservedMask(size) and counts false entries; may round down to multiple of 8; must not double-count or subtract heuristically.\n\nsrc/main/java/QRDetector.java: class QRDetector { public static boolean[] readDataBits(QRMatrix matrix); public static byte[] extractPayloadBytes(QRMatrix matrix); }. Semantics: readDataBits uses FinderPattern.reservedMask and ZigZag.traversal to read all unreserved cells in order; returns possibly non-byte-aligned boolean[]. extractPayloadBytes converts all bits via TextEncoder.bitsToBytes; if fewer than two bytes available, throws IllegalArgumentException; reads unsigned length L from first two bytes; computes exactly int frameLength = 2 + length + 1;; rejects insufficient bytes; literally truncates via byte[] frame = java.util.Arrays.copyOf(bytes, frameLength);; validates via TextEncoder.decodePayload(frame); returns frame. It ignores only zero-padded unused suffix dictated by L and never passes all capacity bytes to decodePayload.\n\nsrc/main/java/QRCodec.java: class QRCodec { public QRMatrix encode(String text); public QRMatrix encode(String text, int size); public String decode(QRMatrix matrix); }. Semantics: encode(String,int) validates size via MatrixValidator.ensureValidSize; computes reserved mask and traversal; encodes frame via TextEncoder.encodePayload; writes TextEncoder.bytesToBits into traversal order; leaves all extra unfilled cells false; calls FinderPattern.place; returns populated matrix. encode(String) auto-sizes literally starting with int size = 21; increments by 2 until MatrixValidator.capacityBits(size) >= frameBitLength; delegates to encode(String,int). decode verifies finders via FinderPattern.verify, extracts frame via QRDetector.extractPayloadBytes, and returns TextEncoder.decodePayload(frame).\n\nsrc/main/java/QRRenderer.java: class QRRenderer { public static String render(QRMatrix matrix); }. Semantics: returns a deterministic textual representation with exactly one line per matrix row; no IO side-effects.\n\nsrc/main/java/Main.java: class Main { public static void main(String[] args); }. Semantics: Demonstrates encode->render->decode pipeline on a fixed short string like \"hello-世界\"; prints at most one concise line; returns normally.\n\nsrc/test/java/MainTest.java: JUnit 5 tests in default package; imports only org.junit.jupiter.api.Test and static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*. Exactly three tests: (1) Main.main does not throw; (2) literal nine-bit fixture new boolean[]{true,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,true} maps via TextEncoder.bitsToBytes to two bytes whose unsigned values are 128 and 128; TextEncoder.decodePayload(TextEncoder.encodePayload(\"hello-世界\")) round-trips; QRCodec.decode(QRCodec.encode(\"hello-世界\")) round-trips; QRRenderer.render emits exactly one line per matrix row; (3) flipping a copied finder cell [0][0] causes IllegalArgumentException during decode.\n\nAllowed import/include directions: Higher-level classes depend on already-declared lower-level modules: QRCodec depends on TextEncoder, MatrixValidator, ZigZag, FinderPattern, and QRMatrix; QRDetector depends on TextEncoder, ZigZag, FinderPattern; QRRenderer depends on QRMatrix; Main depends on QRCodec and QRRenderer. No circular dependencies. All classes reside in the default package (unnamed), and tests must not import these classes explicitly.",
